// Syllabus — Game Theory & Social Choice (curated course; see collection.js for references)
//
// Sources consulted (Oct 2026):
//   References (tables of contents checked):
//   - Osborne, An Introduction to Game Theory (OUP 2004) — full section list from the author’s page,
//     https://www.economics.utoronto.ca/osborne/igt/toc.html
//   - Maschler, Solan & Zamir, Game Theory — 2nd ed. (CUP 2020) TOC,
//     https://assets.cambridge.org/97811088/25146/toc/9781108825146_toc.pdf (“MSZ §x.y” refs follow
//     the 2nd edition; the 1st edition (2013) lacks ch. 15 on stochastic games, so later chapters shift by one).
//   Courses:
//   - Yale ECON 159 Game Theory (Polak), https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159
//   - Stanford CS364A Algorithmic Game Theory (Roughgarden 2013), https://timroughgarden.org/f13/f13.html
//   - MIT 14.12 Economic Applications of Game Theory (OCW listing)
//   Lists of named results:
//   - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_games_in_game_theory
//   - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_theorems
//
// Scope decisions after comparing with the syllabi already in the atlas: voting rules and voting
// paradoxes, May, Arrow, Sen, Gibbard–Satterthwaite, Black’s median voter theorem, McKelvey, the
// Condorcet jury theorem, power indices and apportionment live in polsci (ch. 1–8); Cournot, Bertrand,
// Stackelberg, Hotelling, Spence signalling, the lemons market, auction formats, second-price
// truthfulness, revenue equivalence and the winner’s curse live in micro (ch. 10, 13); Sperner and Brouwer
// live in top (top-9.9, top-13.11); Gale–Shapley itself and LP duality live in algo (algo-11.14,
// algo-11.19); experimental ultimatum and public-goods behaviour live in behav (ch. 6). This collection
// cites those items and supplies the game-theoretic core around them. Gaps filled from the courses:
// PPAD-completeness of Nash, no-regret learning, price of anarchy and potential games (CS364A),
// correlated and trembling-hand equilibria, purification, Chomp/Hex strategy stealing (MSZ ch. 3),
// Blackwell approachability (MSZ ch. 14), nucleolus and the Talmud bankruptcy problem (MSZ ch. 21),
// fair division. Deliberate omissions: stochastic games, the universal belief space, bargaining set,
// global games, Harsanyi’s utilitarian theorem. Items outside the two references (Lemke–Howson’s
// complexity, Yao, Blotto, Nim, potential games, Braess, price of anarchy, cake cutting, spatial PD,
// stochastic stability) cite the nearest related section.
